        -:    1:// Latency-budget decomposition for the C++ implementation.
        -:    2://
        -:    3:// The load sweep measures an end-to-end operation: a caller builds a request,
        -:    4:// routes it to a shard's bounded queue, blocks on a completion handle, and a
        -:    5:// worker thread executes the operation and writes the result back. That path
        -:    6:// bundles two costs of very different character, and the sweep cannot separate
        -:    7:// them:
        -:    8://
        -:    9://   1. the operation's own work -- a pinned hash lookup, a state-machine guard,
        -:   10://      and a field update, all inside one thread;
        -:   11://   2. the cross-thread submit-and-complete handoff -- an enqueue, a wakeup, a
        -:   12://      context switch, and a completion, none of which is language-specific and
        -:   13://      all of which is charged to the scheduler.
        -:   14://
        -:   15:// Both are measured here on the same host, in one process, with one caller
        -:   16:// thread. ShardCore is the single-threaded operation processor, so calling it
        -:   17:// directly gives (1) with no queue in the path; Service wraps the same core in
        -:   18:// the full threaded machinery, so calling it gives (1) + (2). Their difference
        -:   19:// is the handoff.
        -:   20://
        -:   21:// Both figures are batch means over a long loop rather than per-call medians.
        -:   22:// That is deliberate: a clock read costs tens of nanoseconds, the same order as
        -:   23:// the core operation itself, so per-call timestamps would measure the
        -:   24:// instrument. The decomposition only needs the relative magnitude of the two
        -:   25:// terms, which a batch mean resolves cleanly.
        -:   26://
        -:   27:// The map is preloaded to the same occupancy the sweep runs at, so the hash
        -:   28:// lookup does the same amount of work here as it does there.
        -:   29://
        -:   30:// Mirrors rust/examples/latency_budget.rs operation for operation.
